<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Nothing in this application leaves the device in a backup.

  Two mechanisms, because Android 12 split them and turning off only one is
  the mistake that makes this look done:

    cloud-backup     the copy that goes to the person's Google account
    device-transfer  the copy that goes straight to a new phone

  # Why a messenger refuses both

  What is stored here is the conversation history, the sealed MLS session
  blobs, and since the vault stopped asking for a passphrase, `rotelyx.devicekey`,
  which is the key that opens all of it. A backup takes the lock and the key
  together and puts them somewhere this application does not control.

  An application whose whole argument is that it depends on nobody cannot have
  its plaintext history sitting in somebody else's cloud, and the person it
  belongs to would have no way of knowing it was there.

  # What it costs, said plainly

  Somebody who changes phone loses their history. That is already what this
  protocol promises: forward secrecy means nobody keeps the material to rebuild
  a conversation, so there was never an honest way to carry it across. Export
  from inside the application is the answer for anybody who wants a copy, and
  it is theirs rather than Google's.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <exclude domain="root" />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <exclude domain="root" />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
